---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const siteUrl = 'https://shadowharness.github.io/';
const pageUrl = `${siteUrl}en/security/`;
const title = 'Security & Trust — Shadow';
const description =
  'What Shadow stores and what it never receives, per-user isolation, encryption and keys, and your rights to delete and export — a concrete account of how your conversation data is handled.';
---

<SiteLayout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	pageUrl={pageUrl}
	alternateJa={`${siteUrl}security/`}
	alternateEn={pageUrl}
>
	<main class="doc-page">
		<header class="doc-head">
			<h1>Security &amp; Trust</h1>
			<p class="updated">Updated July 11, 2026</p>
			<p class="lead">
				A conversation log with an AI carries the substance of your work and, inevitably,
				personal information. Shadow's job is to hold that record and turn it into memory —
				so this page explains concretely what we store, how it is separated, who can see what,
				and what you can do with it. One principle runs through all of it:
				<strong>protection should come from structure, not from trusting us to behave.</strong>
			</p>
		</header>

		<aside class="keypoints">
			<h2>Key points</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>The only thing sent to the server is your conversation record. Files, passwords, and API keys never leave your machine</li>
				<li>Your data lives in its own isolated room, and no key exists that opens every room</li>
				<li>The original record stays on your PC, memory notes can be exported anytime, and leaving deletes your room</li>
				<li>Never sold, never used for advertising, never used to train AI</li>
				<li>These commitments are never weakened without advance notice</li>
			</ul>
		</aside>

		<article>
			<section id="what-we-hold">
				<h2>1. What we store — and what we don't</h2>
				<p>
					Shadow's server stores your conversation records with the AI, and the memory notes
					(summaries and indexes) built from them automatically — the material an AI needs to
					recall what you did last time. All of it is yours.
				</p>
				<p>
					That record is the only thing sent to the server. The files on your machine, your
					passwords, your API keys, and your work output are not.
				</p>
				<p>
					The original record also stays on your PC; what the server holds is a copy. Because
					the original is in your hands, leaving Shadow never means losing your history.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="isolation">
				<h2>2. Built so users can't mix</h2>
				<p>
					Most services put everyone's data in one shared store and separate users with access
					permissions. In that design, a single permission bug is enough to expose someone
					else's data.
				</p>
				<p>
					Shadow doesn't use that design. Storage and processing are split into an independent
					room per user, and your data is placed only in yours. Since no one else's data exists
					inside your room, a defect there stays contained inside it — and the memory your AI
					can draw on is the contents of your room, nothing else.
				</p>
				<p>
					The building itself — the server machine and its network entrance — is shared; the
					data storage and the memory processing are not. Each room also has its own key. No
					key exists that opens every room, and none is kept at the entrance: breaking the
					front door yields no keys to the rooms.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="transport">
				<h2>3. Connections and keys</h2>
				<p>All traffic between your PC and the server is encrypted.</p>
				<p>
					Keys (access tokens) are issued per device. If you lose your phone, you revoke that
					device's key immediately; your other devices keep working.
				</p>
				<p>
					The server stores only a fingerprint (hash) of each key, never the key itself — a
					break-in on the server yields no usable keys.
				</p>
				<p>
					Connections always start from your side. There is no path from the server into your
					PC. The operations channel is likewise separate from the user entrance, and
					administrative actions cannot be reached from it.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="operators">
				<h2>4. What operators can access</h2>
				<p>
					Whoever runs the server is technically in a position to reach the data inside it —
					true of any cloud service. Shadow confines the use of that position to this:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Your data is opened only when you ask us to investigate a problem, or when recovering from a system failure requires it</li>
					<li>In either case, we contact you and get your consent before opening it</li>
					<li>Your data is never used for sales or advertising, and never sold or passed to third parties</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section id="ai-summary">
				<h2>5. The AI that builds memory notes</h2>
				<p>
					Turning conversation records into memory notes is done by AI dedicated to
					summarization, under two rules:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>The AI used for this runs under terms where your input is not used to train models</li>
					<li>
						The cost is ours, and we never take your API key — holding it would shift the risk
						of a leak, and the fine print of its contract, onto you
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section id="retention">
				<h2>6. Durable, deletable, portable</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>It can't silently shrink.</strong> The server has no operation that truncates
					or overwrites a stored record — accidents that eat your history are prevented by the
					absence of the capability, not by care. Nightly backups are kept in a separate
					location, by generation.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>You can delete it.</strong> Leave, and your room is deleted with you. Backups
					expire within a retention period stated in the terms of service.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>You can take it out.</strong> Memory notes and summarized conversation ranges
					are exportable at any time — and the full original has been on your PC all along.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="dont-have">
				<h2>7. What we never have</h2>
				<p>
					What we don't hold can't leak. Shadow is designed to never receive information it
					doesn't need.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Your password</strong> — sign-in goes through an external account. Your
						password exists only between you and that provider, and never passes through
						Shadow's servers
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Your name or profile</strong> — not stored. What we receive is an opaque
						identifier that says "same person as last time," nothing more
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Your card details</strong> — they go directly to the payment provider and
						never touch our servers
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					What Shadow does hold: your conversation data, that identifier, one contact email
					address, and a list of connected devices (display name and registration date — the
					minimum needed to cut off just the one you lost). The email address is used for
					incident notices and important announcements only, never marketing.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="incident">
				<h2>8. If something goes wrong</h2>
				<p>
					If we become aware of anything that could affect your data — suspected unauthorized
					access, data loss, a serious failure — we tell the affected users promptly and
					plainly: what happened, and what we fixed.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="changes">
				<h2>9. How these commitments change</h2>
				<p>
					This page moves freely in only one direction: stronger. Weakening or removing a
					commitment will not happen without notifying users in advance.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>
	</main>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
	.doc-page {
		max-width: 780px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5rem) 1.5rem 4.5rem;
	}
	.doc-head h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
		color: #f4fbfd;
	}
	.updated {
		color: #708696;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		margin: 0 0 1.8rem;
	}
	.doc-head .lead {
		color: #c3d0dc;
		font-size: 1.03rem;
		line-height: 1.95;
		margin: 0;
	}
	.doc-head .lead strong,
	article strong {
		color: #eef4f8;
	}
	.keypoints {
		margin: 2.4rem 0 0;
		background: rgba(56, 219, 230, 0.05);
		border: 1px solid rgba(56, 219, 230, 0.2);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 1.4rem 1.7rem;
	}
	.keypoints h2 {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		color: #38dbe6;
		margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
	}
	.keypoints ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
	}
	.keypoints li {
		color: #c3d0dc;
		line-height: 1.85;
		margin: 0.4rem 0;
		font-size: 0.96rem;
	}
	article section {
		border-top: 1px solid rgba(56, 219, 230, 0.1);
		margin-top: 2.8rem;
		padding-top: 2.4rem;
	}
	article h2 {
		font-size: 1.35rem;
		color: #f4fbfd;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}
	article p {
		color: #b9c7d4;
		font-size: 0.99rem;
		line-height: 1.95;
		margin: 0.9rem 0;
	}
	article ul {
		padding-left: 1.25em;
		margin: 0.9rem 0;
	}
	article li {
		color: #b9c7d4;
		font-size: 0.99rem;
		line-height: 1.9;
		margin: 0.5rem 0;
	}
</style>
